  "overview": {
    "text": "An mRNA (messenger RNA) vaccine gives some of the body’s cells temporary instructions for making a chosen antigen—a molecule the immune system can recognize. Unlike vaccines that supply a weakened or inactivated germ, mRNA vaccines supply the message for making an antigen. Cells use it to produce a harmless protein piece; immune defenses respond by making antibodies and activating other immune cells, while immune memory can help the body respond faster after later exposure. COVID-19 vaccines provide a familiar example: their mRNA directs cells to produce a piece of SARS-CoV-2’s spike protein. This approach trains immune defenses without requiring the vaccine to contain the whole virus.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.cdc.gov/covid/vaccines/how-they-work.html",
      "https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/how-do-vaccines-work"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "The mRNA carries instructions; it is not the antigen itself. Cells use the instructions to make a protein or protein piece for the immune system to recognize.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.cdc.gov/covid/vaccines/how-they-work.html",
        "https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/how-do-vaccines-work"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For COVID-19 mRNA vaccines, the instructed protein piece comes from the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.cdc.gov/covid/vaccines/how-they-work.html",
        "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/007775.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "After cells make the protein piece, they break down and remove the mRNA.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.cdc.gov/covid/vaccines/how-they-work.html",
        "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/007775.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The immune response includes antibodies and other immune cells; immune memory can help the body respond faster if it encounters the same pathogen again.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.cdc.gov/covid/vaccines/how-they-work.html",
        "https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/how-do-vaccines-work"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Researchers have studied mRNA vaccine technology for decades, including for diseases beyond COVID-19.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.cdc.gov/covid/vaccines/how-they-work.html"
      ]
    }
  ],
